{
  "$schema": "https://unpkg.com/knip@6/schema.json",
  "ignore": [
    "examples/**",
    // A source `scripts/loadtest-boot.sh` COPIES over a scaffolded project: the
    // workflow-step agent. It imports what a scaffolded project has
    // (`@alexkroman1/aai`, `workflow`, `zod`) and is resolved THERE, not here, so
    // knip reads every import as an unlisted root dependency. The directory
    // carries its own package.json declaring them, which is what satisfies
    // Biome; knip resolves a non-workspace manifest against the root instead.
    // Same reason `examples/**` is above it.
    //
    // `scripts/loadtest-stub-agent/**` was here too and is NOT needed: that
    // agent imports only workspace packages (`@alexkroman1/aai`,
    // `@alexkroman1/aai-runtime` and two of their subpaths), which knip resolves
    // from the root fine. The entry suppressed nothing, and knip reports a
    // redundant ignore as a configuration hint — which is the same
    // dead-config shape as the `.size-limit.json` nobody ran.
    "scripts/loadtest-workflow-agent/**"
  ],
  "ignoreBinaries": [
    "safe-chain",
    // `with-test-pg.mjs` shells out to `supabase status -o env` to resolve the
    // local stack. It is a developer's own CLI (brew/npx), deliberately not a
    // dependency: this repo must not install a 100 MB binary to run its tests,
    // and the script treats an absent one as "no stack resolved" with a printed
    // reason rather than a failure.
    "supabase"
  ],
  "ignoreExportsUsedInFile": true,
  // The "exports" check is ON. It was off repo-wide for a long time because
  // enabling it reported 174 unused exports — but that number came from
  // `includeEntryExports` on the three PUBLISHED packages, which made knip
  // report their entry files' exports (the aai-ui components, createRuntime,
  // the provider factories) as unused. Those are the API: their consumers are
  // templates and user projects knip cannot see, so reporting them is knip
  // being asked the wrong question, not a finding.
  //
  // So `includeEntryExports` is set only on the PRIVATE packages, where every
  // importer is inside this repo and knip therefore sees the whole picture.
  // The published packages keep the check for everything that is NOT reachable
  // from a subpath export — internal helpers that lost their last caller,
  // which is exactly what the check is for and what "exports": excluded was
  // hiding. First run of the split found 8, including a re-export of the
  // project-environment pair that its comment claimed callers were using.
  //
  // "types" stays excluded pending the @internal tagging pass.
  "exclude": [
    "types",
    "optionalPeerDependencies",
    "duplicates"
  ],
  "workspaces": {
    ".": {
      // WHAT A PIPELINE INVOKES, and nothing else.
      //
      // This was `scripts/**/*.{mjs,ts}` — every file in `scripts/` declared an
      // entry point, which means the repo's one "what is unused" tool could
      // never report a dead script. It could not have: an entry point is
      // reachable by definition. Three dead chains were sitting in there
      // (`starter-eval/builtins.mjs` and `starter-eval/tsconfig-ab.mjs`, both
      // reading a `run.json` from a producer that was deleted, and
      // `scripts/aai-dev.sh`, duplicating `pnpm aai`), and they are the same
      // documented dead-config family as the `.size-limit.json` nothing read,
      // the `ls-lint` config no pipeline ran, and the `.turbo` path that never
      // matched `cacheDir`.
      //
      // The old comment justified the recursion with "standalone tools live in
      // their own subdirectory (scripts/starter-eval/), and each file there is
      // run directly rather than imported" — which was true of the two files
      // that turned out to be dead, and false of the third, `expectations.mjs`,
      // which was IMPORTED by `packages/aai-evals` and is now a source file
      // inside it (`starter-expectations.ts`). `scripts/` has no subdirectory
      // of `.mjs` left, which is the end state that reasoning pointed at.
      //
      // A script that is a MODULE (`_*.mjs`, `guard-invariants-rules.mjs`,
      // `artifact-size-format.mjs`) is deliberately absent: knip reaches it
      // through the entry that imports it, and losing its last importer is
      // precisely the finding this glob was suppressing. Add a name here only
      // when a pipeline runs it — that makes the exception a reviewable line.
      "entry": [
        // `scripts/check.sh` used to be listed here and MUST NOT be: it is
        // `scripts/check.mjs` now, and the root `check` script names it, so knip
        // discovers it itself and reports a repeat as a redundant pattern. The
        // `.sh` needed the entry precisely because knip does not look inside a
        // `bash x.sh` invocation.
        "scripts/create-changeset.sh",
        // Everything a root `package.json` script or a vitest `globalSetup`
        // names is discovered by knip itself and must NOT be repeated here —
        // it reports a repeat as a redundant pattern, which is the same
        // reviewability property working in the other direction.
        //
        // `gen-gateway-models.mjs` is the one script a pipeline never runs:
        // `check:gateway-models` shells out to it, by path, at runtime. Manual
        // on purpose — see AGENTS.md, "Two manual diagnostics".
        "scripts/gen-gateway-models.mjs",
        "stryker*.config.mjs"
      ],
      "ignoreDependencies": [
        "@stryker-mutator/api"
      ]
    },
    "packages/aai": {
      "entry": [
        "src/host/_test-utils.ts",
        "**/*.test.ts",
        "**/*.test-d.ts",
        // The capability contracts. Nothing imports the directory and nothing is
        // meant to: a capability root exists so api-contracts.mjs can extract a
        // report for that slice of the authoring API. "Unused" in knip's sense
        // and load-bearing in the only sense that matters here. A `--bump
        // --retain` that writes a compatibility fixture owes an entry for it.
        "src/contracts/entrypoints/*.ts"
      ]
    },
    "packages/aai-runtime": {
      "entry": [
        // Test helpers and fuzz harnesses: imported only by sibling specs, and
        // the fixtures directory is read at runtime by the replay harness.
        "src/_test-utils.ts",
        "**/*-test-utils.ts",
        "src/integration/**/*.integration.test.ts",
        "**/*.test.ts",
        // The capability contracts — same reason as `packages/aai` above.
        "src/contracts/entrypoints/*.ts",
        // The tool fixtures `tools-dir.test.ts` scans. Nothing imports them by
        // name and nothing is meant to — a tool is reached by being IN the
        // directory, which is the property under test.
        "src/fixtures/tool-dirs/**/*.ts"
      ]
    },
    "packages/aai-ui": {
      "entry": [
        "**/*.test.{ts,tsx}",
        "**/*.test-d.ts",
        "src/_react-test-utils.ts",
        // The capability contracts, same as `packages/aai` above: a root exists
        // so api-contracts.mjs can extract a report for that slice of the
        // authoring API.
        "src/contracts/entrypoints/*.ts"
      ]
    },
    "packages/aai-cli": {
      "entry": [
        "**/*.test.ts",
        "src/_test-utils.ts"
      ],
      // NO `ignoreDependencies` for `workflow`, and the entry that used to sit
      // here is worth a note because it came and went twice. It existed for
      // FIXTURE SOURCE — template literals the workflow specs write into a
      // scaffolded project, which resolved the DevKit by walking up into this
      // package's `node_modules`, and which knip cannot see because it reads
      // imports rather than strings. This package no longer DECLARES `workflow`
      // at all (nothing here names the specifier, in source or in a fixture
      // string), so an ignore for it is a redundant pattern knip reports as a
      // configuration hint — the dead-config shape the `scripts/loadtest-stub-agent`
      // note above describes. Restore it with a reason if a fixture ever needs
      // the dependency back.
    },
    // `templates/**` stays ignored so a template's unused EXPORTS never reach
    // the report — they are examples, not a surface anything imports. The
    // `@alexkroman1/aai-ui` entry that used to sit beside it is gone:
    // `template-page-mount.test.ts` globs every `client.tsx` as text to
    // correlate its mount with its agent's `page` field, so knip now sees the
    // import that was always there and the exemption became a lie.
    "packages/aai-templates": {
      "ignore": [
        "templates/**",
        "scaffold/**"
      ],
      // NO `ignoreDependencies` for `@alexkroman1/aai-runtime` any more, and the
      // reason is worth knowing before adding one back. It sat here because
      // `scaffold/server.mjs` — ignored above, shipped to users, and compiled
      // from inside this package by `check:template-types` — was its only
      // importer, so the dependency was unused by knip's reading and
      // load-bearing by the gate's. `templates/simple/agent.eval.test.ts`
      // imports `@alexkroman1/aai-runtime/eval/vitest` now, which knip DOES
      // count, so the entry became a redundant-pattern error. If that eval ever
      // goes away, restore the entry with the paragraph above rather than
      // dropping the dependency: the scaffold still needs it to resolve.
    },
    "packages/aai-guest": {
      "entry": [
        "**/*.test.ts"
      ],
      "includeEntryExports": true,
      // These are declared so a STUDIO WORKSPACE can resolve them, not because
      // this package imports them: they are the third-party half of
      // `WORKSPACE_DEPENDENCIES` (`studio-project-shape.ts`), and declaring them
      // here is what lets `sync-guest-toolchain.mjs` read the version this
      // checkout installed — which is the only thing keeping the baked toolchain
      // and the toolchain the repo builds and tests with from drifting.
      //
      // `workflow` and `@workflow/world-postgres` used to head this list, on the
      // separate ground that they were RESOLVED rather than imported —
      // `rewriteWorkflowImports` called `import.meta.resolve("workflow")` at
      // runtime and the declaration was what put a copy in this package's
      // `node_modules`. Neither is a dependency of this package any more, so both
      // entries became redundant patterns knip reports as configuration hints.
      "ignoreDependencies": [
        "@alexkroman1/aai-ui",
        "@types/react",
        "@types/react-dom",
        "react",
        "react-dom",
        "tailwindcss",
        "xstate"
      ]
    },
    "packages/aai-server": {
      "entry": [
        "**/*.test.ts"
      ],
      "includeEntryExports": true,
      "ignoreBinaries": [
        "modal"
      ]
    },
    "packages/aai-evals": {
      "entry": [
        "**/*.test.ts",
        "**/*.eval.test.ts"
      ],
      "includeEntryExports": true
    },
    "packages/aai-studio-client": {
      "entry": [
        "**/*.test.{ts,tsx}"
      ],
      "includeEntryExports": true
    },
    "docs": {
      // knip's typedoc plugin discovers `typedoc.json` by name and nothing
      // else, so the SECOND config — the markdown one `pnpm docs:md` renders
      // through — was invisible, and the plugin it loads read as an unused
      // dependency. Naming both is what keeps that honest in the direction
      // that matters: drop `typedoc-plugin-markdown` from the config and knip
      // reports the dependency, rather than the config silently rendering
      // without it.
      "typedoc": {
        "config": [
          "typedoc.json",
          "typedoc.markdown.json"
        ]
      }
    },
    "packages/aai-studio-server": {
      "entry": [
        "**/*.test.ts"
      ],
      "includeEntryExports": true
    }
  }
}
